AB  - Coal mine goafs are promising geological sites for CO2 sequestration, yet the influence of moisture content on the microstructural evolution of coal during supercritical CO2 (ScCO2) exposure remains unclear. In this study, coal samples with preset initial moisture contents (0%, 5%, and 10%) were subjected to static ScCO2 treatment to investigate the induced microstructural variations. Untreated dry coal was used as the reference. The microstructural evolution of the coal was characterized via Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy, X-ray diffraction, and low-temperature N2 adsorption. The results indicate that ScCO2 treatment reduces the relative abundance of oxygen-containing functional groups and aliphatic structures. Meanwhile, ScCO2 treatment decreased the interlayer spacing of dry coal microcrystals, accompanied by increases in stacking height and crystallite diameter. Among the tested initial moisture contents, the 5% condition was associated with enhanced ScCO2 penetration and pore accessibility in coal, leading to more pronounced variations in aliphatic structures, aromatic enrichment, and overall microstructural response. Accordingly, the 5% condition exhibited the largest increases in total specific surface area (51.1%) and pore volume (17.9%) relative to the untreated dry coal. At the 10% moisture condition, the formation of a continuous water film obstructed ScCO2 diffusion and weakened the extraction efficiency, bringing microstructural parameters to approach those of the raw coal. These findings demonstrate that initial moisture regulates ScCO2-coal interactions through the effects of fluid transport, adsorption-induced swelling, and pore accessibility, providing mechanistic insight into CO2 sequestration in deep coal mine goafs.
